// hypen:time — clocks and timers as a granted capability, not ambient WASI.
//
// Design notes:
//
// 1. This is the first "stdlib" package added after the storage/state
//    pioneers, and it exists for two reasons beyond convenience:
//    - Least privilege: modules never link wasi:clocks directly, so the
//      host controls the entire time surface.
//    - Determinism: the host can substitute a VIRTUAL clock — `now-ms` /
//      `monotonic-ms` read a counter and `sleep-ms` advances it without
//      real waiting — which makes reducer runs deterministically
//      replayable (record the action log, replay the bug exactly).
//
// 2. `sleep-ms` is `async func`: the guest task suspends through the
//    component event loop, keeping the "await capabilities inline"
//    authoring model. Long timers that outlive a reducer call are NOT this
//    — that remains `Effect::Delay`, which schedules a future dispatch.
//    Hosts may clamp `sleep-ms` to the per-call budget.
//
// 3. Reads are sync funcs: they never block, and keeping them sync spares
//    guests an await on every timestamp.
//
// 4. Like `hypen:storage/local`, this capability is implicitly granted —
//    every v0.2 host links it. It is virtualizable rather than deniable.

package hypen:time@0.2.0;

interface clock {
    /// Milliseconds since the Unix epoch, per the host's wall clock (or the
    /// virtual clock during deterministic replay). Not monotonic — the wall
    /// clock can jump.
    now-ms: func() -> u64;

    /// Milliseconds since an arbitrary fixed origin; never decreases.
    /// Use for measuring durations.
    monotonic-ms: func() -> u64;

    /// Suspend the calling task for at least `ms` milliseconds (virtual
    /// clock: advances the clock and resumes immediately). The per-call
    /// wall-clock budget still applies — a sleep cannot extend it.
    sleep-ms: async func(ms: u64);
}
